    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "The concern you're raising exactly revolves around the risk of causing harm with a high dose, even in the context of seemingly benign symptoms like diarrhea for a short period. While the kidney might appear to function normally, high doses of magnesium, especially on an extended \"self-medication\" basis, can lead to fluid imbalances and subsequent kidney strain over time. Even mild kidney issues from the past can raise the risk of developing serious complications later on unknowingly. The body's excretion mechanisms for excess magnesium are limited, and repeatedly loading your kidneys with excess minerals might deplete other minerals, leading to more severe health issues unrelated to the initial symptoms you're trying to alleviate. Short-term relief is understandable, but your approach could lead to unintended long-term effects. Safer, doctor-recommended options, while possibly more inconvenient in the short term, may prevent future health setbacks. Health care professionals can provide solutions tailored to your medical history and current condition.",
